# African Rain Maker

## Lore

Much of Africa is dry, and droughts can last for years; even the lush regions
follow a rainy season with five to seven dry months, and the rains themselves
bring floods and storms. The rain maker controls, directs and adjusts the
weather of both seasons.

He is one of the traditional men of magic, like the medicine man and priest,
and shares their wish to help people - but he is an independent operative,
allied to many tribes or to none, and can be far more mercenary. Compassionate
rain makers charge what a village can afford, from a meal and a place to sleep
to gems, magic items, livestock or a house kept for them. Many become wealthy
honorary members of several villages. The greedy and cruel are respected but
hated and feared: they may extort payment, walk away from a village that will
not pay, and threaten to turn the weather on anyone who crosses them.

About 70% are male. Most keep their secrets within the family, from father to
son. They study rain cycles, weather, the stars, plants and farming; some are
also thieves and con men, others scientists and healers.

## Equipment

A set of travelling clothes and a set of ceremonial clothes, waterproof boots,
a robe or cloak, a few sacks, backpack, utility belt, canteen, sunglasses, air
filter or gas mask, pocket mirror, string, 40 feet (12 m) of rope or cord,
lighter, tinder box, magnifying glass, binoculars or telescope, diving mask and
snorkel, a month of food rations and personal items.

Weapons include a war club or staff and 1D6 throwing sticks or a pair of
knives. 80% have an energy pistol and/or rifle or other high-tech weapon with
several E-clips, and a rain maker may have a few magic items.

Most young rain makers start with a riding animal of fair quality or a
motorcycle; experienced and wealthy ones keep better animals, pack animals, a
personal vehicle and a cargo vehicle with a driver. They often wear M.D.C.
armor of any type in dangerous places, but seldom during ceremonies.

## GM Notes

The rain maker''s lightning works only while it is raining or storming, and is
at its most dangerous in the hands of an unscrupulous character: the book notes
greedy rain makers blackmail villages with the threat of destructive rain and
lightning. The Dance of Bad Medicine and the Dance of Stones exist to punish
cheats and enemies.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'rain-maker');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'rain-maker';

